#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "3b27595a-5d6d-5ea3-92d2-6de7895c19fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-11153" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/93c82014d308d19ebfc10e7046ac1e8b6cf78017b6bc10e0671f43d22aac996a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8b14a175f7931dda3e3b6afd97c5219c390a0674f147289a257e0e655dcc11f1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9d1a634-f1ef-5130-83ca-f3a555027699" :question/math-academy-id "e-12332" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1a869685bd7163515672154b20c2a41a50b5df320bae30e650d7a6d0b7d44ee1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v) , \\, y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Sketch the region $\\Delta.$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6515550b9a280f423cdc2a2e89fd83014733c0783fdd4b2b2966e6a4dd37492a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/86a240b5c7c1fc945e12da274be7e9cac14c1b5a9add004d52d72390afb39aff.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d7dbc3-7e7e-5c1f-83df-870e7589e46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193926} {:answer/id #uuid "c82bfff7-affe-5d8e-a085-68b9790436b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193927} {:answer/id #uuid "493b80cb-750e-5cd5-8b37-89649aaa3a69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193928} {:answer/id #uuid "5024baa6-d1c7-556a-a434-59feab90505a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193929} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce1675-61a0-5a03-a0c9-a2c8da8d25eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bce1675-61a0-5a03-a0c9-a2c8da8d25eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193930} :answer-field/id #uuid "53e931a0-4ee8-5ca9-9d26-a653abca61cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e90b093e-e3d7-5a59-aa98-97c5665b0473" :question/math-academy-id "q-160414" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44fdb6c4-5db1-5225-acf5-6c057e7df861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326278} {:answer/id #uuid "59a0127c-58f1-59af-b252-8721815768d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326279} {:answer/id #uuid "30baa49c-27f7-5be4-a36e-e4f9739a0877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326280} {:answer/id #uuid "9abe1e9f-e58d-5073-ba46-76f90e5db6e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326281} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac40caf-e9b8-5257-a8fc-9bc5350ae5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9abe1e9f-e58d-5073-ba46-76f90e5db6e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326281} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c9ca47-9a76-55ad-a2d3-88696863f96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160909" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18971ee-978b-58e2-9ad8-abb7fe8ac182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0c093c8b5b55bcf5c50b0fc6c60d47256c9aaf86b8114ba29d6cef5def8cd310.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326285} {:answer/id #uuid "31199d1f-63d8-5127-acd2-01ae2efc4ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/36cd7eaa8f2be8e3c8be883f7de52c2ea8bb87b2ac40b6ae0f9de22598d53ef6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326286} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc8deb8-74be-5430-81fd-3b519d28f4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ce5b81c795019bb779f766d70a42872ef15aac38440a4c38876c76842b3a0c69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326287} {:answer/id #uuid "16b17410-589b-5856-9cd9-a4b18b20809a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/993c8929f019f9753f8b9e0b6662248bf9f41b8f743d423ef11c4838922de17c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326288} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7dad81-7a6f-507f-a9ad-a6318d00080d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7dad81-7a6f-507f-a9ad-a6318d00080d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326289} :answer-field/id #uuid "01624f8b-38d3-5349-8d8e-b6f320f0a941" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62187b1b-2a1e-5c4c-8542-39321690d816" :question/math-academy-id "q-160912" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f436ee381cafdb41a4a8df2bc68c06751d7d0a6d55641303caf04dcb4cab6619.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d79c9166-24ff-51a2-b424-c41a56512d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5adf324eba50123bd9a6dc3f27be38a7db0b4835bac8479e705f696abcf404b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326292} {:answer/id #uuid "fd09bd09-4823-57ce-9a6f-39e3e8ceeb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326293} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf9d8df-a42b-5788-add4-85bba83b71a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5263c0c82be5ab6b0290e5017943c7c2584d3bd23783c5420e38af2df618f9b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326294} {:answer/id #uuid "2a301fbe-ac55-53bc-9fd4-1320aea78a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0799c923b5e7061f8a6ec1faf9ed8b73038dfe4c1b63607a8e037d66d6ce88fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326295} {:answer/id #uuid "74e1f549-07b2-5d9a-a0da-1f907b9ba356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/25ec9c36f1e6637a0034cdae142977b4a4b5c3237471ef93f4bf55c6f4ea86b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd09bd09-4823-57ce-9a6f-39e3e8ceeb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326293} :answer-field/id #uuid "70815d54-9fff-5916-8240-6134755ba69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdd9fff8-ea20-509c-a6da-935754b92421" :question/math-academy-id "q-160914" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3621e70552c3891f43521e74704d5db3f276f9928669390d688cca1bc3449217.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c8ad4cf64e9ea17b225373d8ed238c8588c0b6f45577d2ca15b22ede5e34d7d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c7f5dad-2fe1-5a07-843c-3a43aceb26ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2eb8b5f7ee3fe1b85960f1d6c87a2aabf58aae6f97394b2c7145cc98b39708dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326299} {:answer/id #uuid "f98c57d6-89c8-5beb-91fb-4af971d684de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1c9d7de1e895affd9f75deda09fe4b3243ea39a04b47198309e0b52c6197b2f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326300} {:answer/id #uuid "4bee19fa-fd0d-5461-84f9-735b5469f449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/37bccae9033e5eac9a48f8d3d570d69d1a382f26646617665c75f32a9ac7ab4e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326301} {:answer/id #uuid "d328ec07-3a06-5f8c-85d3-ce5788889ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2e8246c7764ee6a059da534fa078f3d3c7caebfbc55554be2da63002da2007bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326302} {:answer/id #uuid "223a1962-97f9-56cd-b92d-58f591f99e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "223a1962-97f9-56cd-b92d-58f591f99e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326303} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd02f42-7fd6-51a2-a403-8b5990413165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa799b6-3a6e-5218-a18f-25be6930dc00" :question/math-academy-id "q-160925" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4f7035-4638-56bf-b95a-0a8f4a672bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326306} {:answer/id #uuid "a54f7e81-557a-58af-bab5-b17a483c6d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9f3fd1164ab4346961dab3ab258cf06ccd7aea62644375feeb2b24690c3ca718.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326307} {:answer/id #uuid "c008f5f8-c68a-5437-9f84-03a1b21ede2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa9471f-2005-56b5-886b-1c8e430ac917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/49ea0e312d1572a3e598ae1962fac6fd10e61835b40c73310792857eb0dc2380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326309} {:answer/id #uuid "7071b572-26fd-547f-b29d-798968e64b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3b09b73c500e5c08dd443f1a53f8fbc1a644d18bcd90aa3e96bc1a02e77db75b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4f7035-4638-56bf-b95a-0a8f4a672bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326306} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cc789c-512d-50e8-b212-43c0683563d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfb7c239-a64d-500a-8d8c-d627867333a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160942" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0412da40e0e04e8da5ec92db1ad665251209a718333ec008ceeebbe308c71855.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=0$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0,\\,0),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d31bfe-9f36-5285-958c-5b70eb2bad9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326313} {:answer/id #uuid "fb52a5b5-c1b3-5c55-99da-fd0c87170494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/e8ed422a10846fc3f44fb894d1051f184dde24b5a38181f25366ba209c5986c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326314} {:answer/id #uuid "66b3a447-46c3-5695-8fa2-c58df3df739a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326315} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1f50d8-2a27-5872-bf3a-0a203734273c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/772ffafe5ebbe9aad24464f1a4ee7937c2a14895f62d6a73e0f1422a0bc899ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326316} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a0d58a-0583-5d6f-b396-1eea0cd9f441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/df47e830c98a8f5e3ddfdf909ec23163735b21486eb25d784bb6b88ea95e4f60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d31bfe-9f36-5285-958c-5b70eb2bad9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326313} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22c476f-51db-5105-92dd-f2b050563dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0c66a8-0dcc-5684-892c-2aa4d891f439" :question/math-academy-id "q-160944" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0355357-5b11-5cce-b549-78d9d67bf027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2be15ae2d8bf7a309065ac33c97f5937d3fc8c12c5ca6ddc0c54ab44f555a5d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326320} {:answer/id #uuid "f260913c-e8e6-5ece-83d6-e582ae54cada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326321} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7b0c92-03dc-5d3a-943b-49ba8f714b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/549d8c8e2e6cf9eb785c0c0f80ddfe95bfdb51429a6797368b97908241ef713c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326322} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dece5a-40cc-576f-80d0-0ca8ea25b267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9a40e3dbc6eeecaf9b33830b762e75f54d7ffea5c1dd9ab9ae6e2bd9cca721e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326323} {:answer/id #uuid "690d666e-d29a-5566-931e-adabf743b927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b611546ee3a8ae2714de1cc97dec62b9c750a39f858864b63d70be0d26a65b68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e7b0c92-03dc-5d3a-943b-49ba8f714b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/549d8c8e2e6cf9eb785c0c0f80ddfe95bfdb51429a6797368b97908241ef713c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326322} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b7eadf-c003-5ebd-b5a5-951ed22edb4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a00f14bc-7f15-569a-bc33-e10f7eaf55f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160945" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2932639855f3031da6adcca7eaee38b992a145809f119902e93958fee47ff2a6.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97156748-1072-5619-9e28-cbf3b45c29ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326327} {:answer/id #uuid "deab7343-7128-5d0b-8d29-13c412baf5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/67efdc05dfb99f81f4d612302e44546c114d84fc7e7b64e068c37b2d99ed6727.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326328} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5a14ae-b17f-51e5-86b6-9dd42f164a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9a6f6dbe77adc95ad0ff833651fc22e2c68e5f0e541b65bc6ce7dcf7b7b41f4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326329} {:answer/id #uuid "449456b2-8657-5494-9617-700bcde3a06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/59b8cc960c2a923a31ba1dfa8fd62d7b5697bc152623e6f295efb65bea2f6741.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326330} {:answer/id #uuid "826a7b81-031e-59f5-a3f1-4b2adf5cc124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97156748-1072-5619-9e28-cbf3b45c29ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326327} :answer-field/id #uuid "3238333d-bae4-5f6e-aad0-0be94b67d2f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29377ed0-f103-5d84-ae34-1182af962bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-160948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/930509fb9224152be07ef3e14550e0f7afe803428a357963eb3796e0650685a3.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158c25ae-0fec-5f92-aeee-6758d781029b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/e42a5506af4f1d8638dc09938217bb1f6e059d94945af11c847b63753ed768c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326334} {:answer/id #uuid "9776820f-0d74-553d-bc56-faafddc977fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/7feba2657a73ffe233cb78cdc8b63634c3af67ab64e517f1ae97319c89b0e757.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326335} {:answer/id #uuid "b1184bc5-5861-52ef-854f-7c40821f03d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/4440fc795798064ebaec0a41ec89d156ab16e86672b7d467acb197c50acfb0f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326336} {:answer/id #uuid "06531186-d811-5ca1-9c07-9ccc1e319bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f9ebd2bd9d011bfa60660434993c09e9047293026dc9d709212b34d8aa659a6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326337} {:answer/id #uuid "83fb0483-e35b-5fc1-9062-f43c17191226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/733a8ba79352dcae279b78f841e4439af4eaa1d243506612745a44774ef55aa0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06531186-d811-5ca1-9c07-9ccc1e319bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f9ebd2bd9d011bfa60660434993c09e9047293026dc9d709212b34d8aa659a6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326337} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adde19a-eff3-5f47-b95c-3343f20fdf12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4857ee0f-58dd-5287-9162-0826380416b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160951" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81ccd8830bcafa56065d5f7481e0d0b1dc114d0cd67a9d04220c8bb4459968d3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=0$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/968e682dbaa9d8bdb95808e609a1dba6c68e8e12d9cae1abb59755b5ec640286.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30019e9a-2f0e-5671-83f0-449ffbffc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326341} {:answer/id #uuid "66f9a9c2-3218-5626-b3fa-332d1f01b1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9bfcd7c758af688acfdec1ce287838470539fbb2fea728a54810f08193b5246a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326342} {:answer/id #uuid "9660a404-2a1f-5432-b51b-e785305446e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/59fbcb01193bfc07615f1f321a5417c1ad9c2819a9befc9a2745e73f791f6e48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326343} {:answer/id #uuid "beddf03d-f188-50cd-9eef-6e4f81b27f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326344} {:answer/id #uuid "b7eb7338-80f1-5626-ad57-f908db367e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1e08a502271231c6594e3f6924d5d8791c1ff2f4a9988d18d3e8c0cecde3ee92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30019e9a-2f0e-5671-83f0-449ffbffc1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326341} :answer-field/id #uuid "552f736a-c3fa-57d2-a1f6-4ffbd6f94aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cef91c5c-6d4e-5d54-a2c1-586a57e9523c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160967" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/21952fe273fd1a1ecb03727f4aa254d4e0ecdab2feaeb1d8c652bc9923404846.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the third quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c847e93-968e-5ee2-ab48-0db10ea97c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/e50d218c70d9f4f5e77a9ef53b5fb2712b6b400450fc2165e7972b06621b9757.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326348} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7e3ad4-bb40-5baa-97de-71a64ce1a53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326349} {:answer/id #uuid "1d480341-5bb5-5579-b875-957e826bf886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/aafdff659d5c73ab37dfe9cf27c8777d03d7731f04dd106c149e6b4bfbc45fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326350} {:answer/id #uuid "c04370dd-6a5c-5385-a0cc-00706bd20b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351} {:answer/id #uuid "9f504099-7786-5b56-9fc8-90ea68cb2d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/399dc3c9750ed7e041f1973cad0a24a18903360ba68f0dbcb42c566436c9e259.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d7e3ad4-bb40-5baa-97de-71a64ce1a53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326349} :answer-field/id #uuid "417b0651-eb91-5de7-af46-d6ede2243243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7dffa96-ac1f-5c9d-b16b-8ea8f986a8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161025" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/de9f7a32a41bcecc1707b90d6818d58473473a4dc26d17e39e957461de95d671.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ce25d4-ed17-55ec-ab3c-d89f301eaab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326355} {:answer/id #uuid "be8102ce-d018-5bee-a57e-7fcfb32c8a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326356} {:answer/id #uuid "76249444-4841-59c7-bcf6-835606a28450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/cbcba98cd11ce740be941848d0d9d4524c8cbc58d6de7aee0cfb2f7421c457db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326357} {:answer/id #uuid "94cd4ae7-2fb7-561a-abcd-0e59c3eee05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/deafbae15e6d30f175122d3662397f05398d31579b80520648accaf1fff93e85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326358} {:answer/id #uuid "1a45165c-c790-5478-b43f-8e172433f179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/13810a874a27ea2db773ed90fa02f5b1b833d90372b38a3273f416b30d9a1a1f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72ce25d4-ed17-55ec-ab3c-d89f301eaab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326355} :answer-field/id #uuid "39402eae-c833-5d43-815e-6e0dfad5e93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "855b4a2f-2e33-50bd-8a10-858d1cf8b642" :question/math-academy-id "q-161026" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/708bf4df2d45383b0dd367f5bfe3dc9e260a17cfefe0566e2b6d37dec98af2ef.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $0\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})),\\,0\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed8cc6a3-b85b-5811-9483-f7eac8fd76f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2d95ce1c0b844b49000b05ef44ef0da8b516740804a8e6bd275a1f9ca15b2ed0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326362} {:answer/id #uuid "075f3eb3-3f6b-5eb0-8457-44213eb29188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a63ab6b0cb9e525901e8173ae54afb89530ae96342975731b76e51b2a51d752e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326363} {:answer/id #uuid "31f13522-703d-5347-90a4-ac4c31e3ccf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/539932b6d0bc70809848bf1690df34dc56ebb569dc4877c231bd08e8698c5764.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326364} {:answer/id #uuid "f18cda0c-0738-5cfa-856e-ddcfa6a9ce4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326365} {:answer/id #uuid "436a9d83-28be-54fd-b142-8521492c4290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18cda0c-0738-5cfa-856e-ddcfa6a9ce4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326365} :answer-field/id #uuid "3faa8fe0-2805-5f5c-a1c0-59317f76260b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28df611d-b4f1-50bf-9ab3-8cd1c3286161" :question/math-academy-id "q-161028" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e05fcd1e-0f8f-574f-9c83-47b9a035b47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326369} {:answer/id #uuid "113ec32c-637b-5295-94e3-24b0e629d7a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326370} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ebd0ca-4372-55c1-bddd-5f2ada13fa42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326371} {:answer/id #uuid "cea40ff5-125b-55e2-ab20-8e0c07318226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326372} {:answer/id #uuid "d53e305f-4453-5bc0-8e90-95ba72600102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "113ec32c-637b-5295-94e3-24b0e629d7a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326370} :answer-field/id #uuid "f060482f-8518-56a6-850c-f026a266ac3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc24e98f-4c39-5479-a484-21b2eaa8a13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161031" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0963fc3c56b5baac68e2d587896bf4655950640ecce5b924edc2d8e26a3e64e2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(u,\\,0),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/92bd315a07a5d76e77c249c12ea4544ee1555b0c214c68cf1f92fc3f07a71ddf.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $0$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point \n$(0,\\,0),$\nwhich is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies on the circle with radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe8265c-558c-56f5-a30a-15de3b13cd40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326376} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bab996-f996-5847-8234-1d847b915573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326377} {:answer/id #uuid "516d43a6-002e-5ed9-aecc-54f9a0fd2a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326378} {:answer/id #uuid "27684675-c376-5bb0-99aa-74fbecda7e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326379} {:answer/id #uuid "d955ac6a-3174-55aa-97c9-6fa15e14011c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d955ac6a-3174-55aa-97c9-6fa15e14011c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326380} :answer-field/id #uuid "28392b5c-1b32-5346-bbc0-aebc06dc938a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff953a39-6efa-54c4-a86e-77d5c7de4aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161035" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8afe0884ccf1e62ffdfef6cab4626bc59b3138aa5cd26f855980c88971753447.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2c5f48-6466-5dec-b5fc-4fc898ebadba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/83d410ca6de599371a47574cf2c6c3fa659f89dca32c6aadd5596276c3ef0c9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326383} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff9ab7e-c3c4-5221-9b29-873980c68baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6fd834262bbe36779afee02caa6df26520fb4d7e3c451806411e7c2a34ba0ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326384} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f1ba33-5d37-59ac-bab3-de407ff203ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/aaa000e6d8798b9f62cc3afc814931378dee78f1527e46befb5cfd51b0a00249.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326385} {:answer/id #uuid "c39356a0-f33e-565f-85e7-e7e6e7c65bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/4d1341dc970cd8a7c52ee7e0988e40f40254ddb8dee77b7dcba2010a126f9fe0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326386} {:answer/id #uuid "139aee71-9d6c-51b2-89a2-679073a30f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "139aee71-9d6c-51b2-89a2-679073a30f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326387} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe10ddb1-e1e7-5de2-aff1-f7a32d9a9fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6f6478-43eb-5df8-9592-d3846d859923" :question/math-academy-id "q-161113" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/22ed424a27d1674bc7f4316b0b644e3dbac4c9f460e23ea2aa6ee90cb74b4c5b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da02431e-ab19-527d-b937-a44c2612e5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326390} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3b93af-0519-541c-ac96-a773e2b6fbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81d08b5c3f511d6a51c1fc01d40ef25cf52b1fc8f10c055f924425aab7f9e39c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326391} {:answer/id #uuid "60c20a58-d91b-57e2-91fb-660b98740d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8f0573ca9a95fc277d1993e08b7307d2a9e8be48b80e1ea2360f9fc688b440b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326392} {:answer/id #uuid "f8725c78-f13c-5f58-aeb8-6c796e9619b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/89f8cd5fdb19ae5a3eb74bb1d519da855ea6f32027aa3e536c8cea99228c13cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326393} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e54140-3e55-5b2e-a35d-bb2983992f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8a8b84b7858341f49fdba